{"status": "progress", "message": "Arbitration completed: Final verdict 'CONTRADICTED'", "stage": "arbiter", "claim_index": 2, "data": {"kind": "arbiter", "mode": "review", "label": "contradicted", "error": null, "reasoning": "[PERSPECTIVE A: DEFENSE (Maximal Support)]\nThe claim that Albert Einstein won a Nobel Prize is partially supported by Evidence 3, which states, \"he won the prize two years later\" (referring to the period following his 1919 divorce). If one interprets the claim's date \"1980\" as a potential typographical error or a reference to a posthumous recognition or a different category often conflated with his name, one could argue the evidence acknowledges he is a Nobel laureate. The text confirms his receipt of \"the prize\" in a historical context related to his divorce settlement.\n\n[PERSPECTIVE B: PROSECUTION (Maximal Skepticism)]\nThe claim is demonstrably false based on a lack of temporal and categorical grounding. First, Evidence 3 establishes that Einstein won a prize shortly after 1919 (historically the 1921 Nobel Prize in Physics), which contradicts the date \"1980\" by over six decades. Second, the claim specifies the \"Nobel Prize in Literature,\" while the evidence regarding Einstein in Evidence 3 refers to the Nobel Prize in Physics (implied by the timeline of his scientific career). Finally, Evidence 1 defines the Nobel Prize in Literature as a prize for \"an author\" for \"work in an idealistic direction,\" a category for which there is no evidence Einstein was either nominated or selected. The claim misidentifies both the category of the prize and the timeline of the event.\n\n[PERSPECTIVE C: JUDICIAL ARBITRATION]\nThe claim asserts two specific attributes: that Albert Einstein won a Nobel Prize in *Literature* and that this occurred in *1980*. \n\n1. **Category Discrepancy:** Evidence 1 defines the Nobel Prize in Literature as a literary award for authors. Historical consensus and the context of Einstein's life (Evidence 3) confirm his Nobel Prize was awarded for Physics. The claim incorrectly assigns a literary honor to a physicist.\n2. **Temporal Discrepancy:** The claim cites 1980. Evidence 3 indicates Einstein won \"the prize\" two years after 1919, placing the actual event in 1921. \n3. **Application of Rules:** Per the Arbitration instructions, \"If the claim refers to an entity by a name, title, date or attribute that is wrong... treat the claim as CONTRADICTED.\" Because the claim misidentifies the field (Literature vs. Physics) and the date (1980 vs. 1921), the claim is factually false relative to the evidence provided.\n\nVERDICT: CONTRADICTED"}, "t": 18.161}
{"status": "claim_result", "index": 2, "result": {"claim": "Albert Einstein won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1980.", "evidence": "Evidence 1: The Nobel Prize in Literature, here meaning for Literature (Swedish: Nobelpriset i litteratur), is a Swedish literature prize that is awarded annually to an author from any country who has, in the words of Alfred Nobel, \"in the field of literature, produced the most outstanding work in an idealistic direction\". Though individual works are sometimes cited as being particularly noteworthy, the award is based on an author's body of work as a whole. The Swedish Academy decides who, if anyone, will receive the prize. The academy announces the name of the laureate in early October. It is one of the five Nobel Prizes established by the will of Alfred Nobel in 1895.\n\nEvidence 2: However, since 2016, the International Booker Prize now recognises an annual book of fiction translated into English. Previous winners of the International Booker Prize who have gone on to win the Nobel Prize in Literature include Alice Munro, Olga Tokarczuk, and Han Kang. The Neustadt International Prize for Literature is regarded as one of the most prestigious international literary prizes, often referred to as the American equivalent of the Nobel Prize. Like the Nobel Prize, it is awarded not for any one work but for an entire body of work. It is frequently seen as an indicator of who may be awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature.\n\nEvidence 3: In letters that Einstein wrote to Marie Winteler in the months before Eduard's arrival, he described his love for his wife as \"misguided\" and mourned the \"missed life\" that he imagined he would have enjoyed if he had married Winteler instead: \"I think of you in heartfelt love every spare minute and am so unhappy as only a man can be.\" In 1912, Einstein entered into a relationship with Elsa L\u00f6wenthal, who was both his first cousin on his mother's side and his second cousin on his father's. When Mari\u0107 learned of his infidelity soon after moving to Berlin with him in April 1914, she returned to Zurich, taking Hans Albert and Eduard with her. Einstein and Mari\u0107 were granted a divorce on 14 February 1919 on the grounds of having lived apart for five years. As part of the divorce settlement, Einstein agreed that if he were to win a Nobel Prize, he would give the money that he received to Mari\u0107; he won the prize two years later.", "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_Einstein, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nobel_Prize_in_Literature", "verification": {"label": "contradicted", "confidence": 0.8998112082481384, "probabilities": [0.8998112082481384, 0.09488917887210846, 0.005299527198076248], "logits": [2.368, 0.1185, -2.7666], "evidence_scores": {"decided_by": 2, "snippets": [{"source": "wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nobel_Prize_in_Literature", "label": "unknown", "probabilities": [0.4764295518398285, 0.5234137773513794, 0.00015668147534597665], "logits": [2.6513, 2.7454, -5.3685], "decisive": false}, {"source": "wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nobel_Prize_in_Literature", "label": "contradicted", "probabilities": [0.7922325134277344, 0.2076094150543213, 0.00015800714027136564], "logits": [3.322, 1.9828, -5.198], "decisive": false}, {"source": "wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_Einstein", "label": "contradicted", "probabilities": [0.8998112082481384, 0.09488917887210846, 0.005299527198076248], "logits": [2.368, 0.1185, -2.7666], "decisive": true}], "S": 0.0, "K": 0.8998112082481384, "n_sup": 0, "n_con": 1, "case": "review", "shared_role": false, "tau": 0.8, "m": 2}, "conformal_set": ["contradicted"], "coverage_guarantee": "90%", "is_escalation_candidate": false, "conformal_p_values": {"contradicted": 0.2871, "unknown": 0.099, "supported": 0.0396}, "conformal_q_hat": 0.898471511900425, "set_size": 1, "final_decision_policy": "contradiction_arbitration", "debate_resolved": true, "tr